---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

import ArticleFeedback from "../../components/ArticleFeedback.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Why Your Best Dating Photo Isn't Always the Best One to Lead With"
    description="Your favorite dating photo may be the most flattering one you have, but that doesn't always make it the best first photo. Here's what the first image actually needs to do."
>
    <main class="article-page">
        <article>
            <header class="article-header">
                <p class="eyebrow">DATING PROFILE PHOTOS</p>

                <h1>
                    Why Your Best Dating Photo Isn't Always the Best One to Lead
                    With
                </h1>

                <p class="article-intro">
                    Your favorite dating photo may be the most flattering one
                    you have, but that doesn't always make it the best first
                    photo. The first image has a different job from simply
                    making you look your best.
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="article-content">
                <p>You finally find the photo.</p>

                <p>
                    The lighting is good. You like your expression. You look
                    like yourself. Maybe it's the most flattering picture you've
                    taken in a long time.
                </p>

                <p>So naturally, it goes first.</p>

                <p>But there is another question worth asking.</p>

                <p>Not:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>Is this my best photo?</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>But:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>Is this the best place to start?</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>Those aren't always the same thing.</p>

                <h2>The first photo has a different job</h2>

                <p>
                    A dating profile is experienced in sequence. Someone sees
                    one photograph before the others, and that first image
                    becomes the starting point for everything that follows.
                </p>

                <p>
                    It doesn't need to explain your personality. It doesn't need
                    to show every interesting thing about your life. It doesn't
                    even need to be the most memorable photograph in the
                    profile.
                </p>

                <p>
                    It just needs to make the rest of the profile easier to
                    understand.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That's a slightly different job from simply being the most
                    flattering photograph.
                </p>

                <h2>
                    A beautiful photo can still give very little information
                </h2>

                <p>
                    Imagine a photograph where you look fantastic. The light is
                    beautiful. Your expression is great. Everything is
                    technically right.
                </p>

                <p>But the image gives almost no sense of context.</p>

                <p>
                    Someone can see your face, but there isn't much else to
                    read. They know what you look like in that particular
                    photograph, but they haven't learned much about the person
                    behind it.
                </p>

                <p>That doesn't make the photograph bad.</p>

                <p>It just means its role may be different.</p>

                <p>
                    Another photograph might be slightly less polished but give
                    a clearer sense of who they're looking at. That can make it
                    a stronger starting point.
                </p>

                <h2>The first photo can create a question</h2>

                <p>
                    This doesn't mean the first photograph needs to be
                    mysterious or clever. You don't need to make someone solve a
                    puzzle before they can understand your profile.
                </p>

                <p>
                    It can simply leave room for the other photographs to add
                    something.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Maybe the first image clearly shows your face. The second
                    shows what you like doing. The third gives some sense of
                    your everyday surroundings.
                </p>

                <p>Now the photographs are working together.</p>

                <p>The first one doesn't have to carry the entire profile.</p>

                <p>It opens the door.</p>

                <h2>Flattering and useful are different qualities</h2>

                <p>We often use one question to judge a dating photo:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>Do I look good?</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>It's an important question. It just isn't the only one.</p>

                <p>
                    A useful profile photograph can also feel easy to understand
                    and believable. You should be recognizable in it, and it
                    should feel consistent with the rest of your profile. Most
                    importantly, it should look like a version of you that makes
                    sense outside the photo too.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A photograph can be extremely flattering and still not be
                    very useful as the first thing someone sees. The best first
                    photo isn't necessarily the one that makes you look the most
                    impressive. It's the one that makes the rest of the profile
                    easier to understand.
                </p>

                <p>And a simpler photograph can sometimes do more work.</p>

                <h2>Your first photo shouldn't feel like an advertisement</h2>

                <p>
                    There is a subtle difference between presenting yourself
                    well and trying to sell yourself.
                </p>

                <p>
                    When a photograph is trying too hard to communicate
                    everything at once, it can start to feel more like an
                    advertisement than a person.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That's especially easy to do with the first photo because it
                    feels important. You know someone is going to see it first.
                </p>

                <p>
                    So you start looking for the perfect expression, the perfect
                    outfit, the perfect background.
                </p>

                <p>
                    And suddenly the photograph is carrying a lot of pressure.
                </p>

                <p>It may look polished.</p>

                <p>But it can become less personal.</p>

                <p>
                    You don't want the first image to feel like you're
                    submitting a headshot for approval.
                </p>

                <p>You're giving someone a first glimpse of you.</p>

                <h2>Think about what comes next</h2>

                <p>
                    One useful way to choose your first photograph is to stop
                    looking at it by itself.
                </p>

                <p>Look at the second photograph. Then the third.</p>

                <p>
                    Ask yourself whether the first image makes those photographs
                    feel like natural additions.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If the first picture already tells the entire story, the
                    others can feel unnecessary.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If the first picture tells almost nothing, the profile may
                    feel confusing.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The best starting point is often somewhere between those
                    two.
                </p>

                <p>Enough information to understand the person.</p>

                <p>Enough room for the rest of the profile to add something.</p>

                <h2>The goal isn't to win the first photograph</h2>

                <p>It's to make the whole profile easier to experience.</p>

                <p>
                    Your best dating profile doesn't necessarily begin with the
                    photograph that makes you look the most impressive.
                </p>

                <p>
                    It begins with a photograph that feels like a believable
                    introduction.
                </p>

                <p>Then the rest can do what one photograph never could.</p>

                <p>Show a little more of the person.</p>

                <p>Not everything.</p>

                <p>
                    Just enough to make the profile feel less like a collection
                    of pictures and more like a glimpse of an actual life.
                </p>

                <p>
                    So when you're choosing the first photo, don't automatically
                    reach for the one you think is your absolute best.
                </p>

                <p>Look at the whole profile instead.</p>

                <p>
                    Ask whether that photograph makes you easier to understand,
                    whether it feels believable, and whether it gives the next
                    photograph somewhere to go.
                </p>

                <p>Your favorite photo can still be the favorite.</p>

                <p>It just doesn't have to be first.</p>
            </div>

            <ArticleFeedback
                article="why-your-best-dating-photo-isnt-always-the-best-one-to-lead-with"
            />
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .article-page {
        width: min(100% - 48px, var(--container));

        margin: 0 auto;
    }

    .article-header {
        max-width: 820px;

        padding: 120px 0 100px;
    }

    .article-header h1 {
        margin: 0;

        max-width: 820px;

        font-size: clamp(48px, 6vw, 76px);

        line-height: 1;

        font-weight: 500;

        letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    }

    .article-intro {
        max-width: 600px;

        margin: 32px 0 0;

        font-size: 20px;

        line-height: 1.55;

        color: var(--color-muted);
    }

    .article-content {
        max-width: 680px;

        padding: 80px 0 160px;

        border-top: 1px solid rgba(36, 36, 36, 0.12);
    }

    .article-content p {
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem;

        font-size: 19px;

        line-height: 1.75;
    }

    .article-content h2 {
        max-width: 680px;

        margin: 5rem 0 1.5rem;

        font-size: clamp(30px, 4vw, 42px);

        line-height: 1.08;

        font-weight: 500;

        letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    }

    .article-content ul {
        margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;

        padding-left: 1.5rem;
    }

    .article-content li {
        margin-bottom: 0.65rem;

        font-size: 19px;

        line-height: 1.65;
    }

    .article-content blockquote {
        margin: 2rem 0;

        padding-left: 1.5rem;

        border-left: 2px solid rgba(36, 36, 36, 0.18);
    }

    .article-content blockquote p {
        margin: 0;

        font-size: 21px;

        line-height: 1.6;
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
        .article-page {
            width: min(100% - 32px, var(--container));
        }

        .article-header {
            padding: 72px 0 80px;
        }

        .article-header h1 {
            font-size: clamp(42px, 11vw, 58px);
        }

        .article-intro {
            font-size: 18px;
        }

        .article-content {
            padding: 56px 0 100px;
        }

        .article-content p {
            font-size: 17px;

            line-height: 1.7;
        }

        .article-content li {
            font-size: 17px;

            line-height: 1.7;
        }

        .article-content h2 {
            margin-top: 4rem;

            font-size: 32px;
        }

        .article-content blockquote p {
            font-size: 19px;
        }
    }
</style>
